{"hash":"6401c5050b0e9fed","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":87.488,"said":"Back in the court, stand before the temple’s southern steps and look up. Red basalt, carved from plinth to spire: elephants along the base, gods in their niches, and higher up, the ten avatars of Vishnu among scenes of Shiva’s own stories. The Lord stayed here for Ghushma, but his house has had to be raised more than once. In the wars of the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries the old temple was damaged, and for long years after it was rebuilt and damaged again. Then, in the late sixteenth century, a Maratha commander named Maloji Bhosale restored it. Maloji was the grandfather of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, and Verul is remembered as the ancestral village of the Bhosale family; their memorials stand near this temple. Tradition says Maloji found a hidden treasure, and spent it on this temple and on a great tank for Shiva at Shikhar Shingnapur. The temple you see is mostly the gift of the next century, and of a queen: Ahilyabai Holkar of Indore, who restored temples from Kashi to Somnath. Most accounts credit her with raising this one in its present form. Now climb the steps into the hall. Nandi is waiting there.\n\nDid you know? The Shiva Purana places Ghushmesha near Devagiri; the great hill fort of Devagiri, today’s Daulatabad, stands about fifteen kilometres away.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1130},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1146,"end":1286}],"words":[[0.098,0.361,0,4],[0.471,0.072,5,7],[0.555,0.084,8,11],[0.651,0.385,12,17],[1.204,0.397,19,24],[1.613,0.3,25,31],[1.925,0.084,32,35],[2.021,0.505,36,44],[2.538,0.373,45,53],[2.923,0.433,54,59],[3.367,0.192,60,63],[3.572,0.18,64,68],[3.764,0.216,69,71],[4.904,0.252,73,76],[5.168,0.613,77,83],[5.986,0.264,85,91],[6.262,0.228,92,96],[6.502,0.3,97,103],[6.815,0.096,104,106],[6.923,0.517,107,112],[7.656,0.541,114,123],[8.209,0.312,124,129],[8.534,0.084,130,133],[8.63,0.445,134,138],[9.267,0.385,140,144],[9.